%%
%% SECTION 9: SYNTHESIS
%%
\section{Synthesis and Discussion}
\label{sec:synthesis}

Read together, the literature converges on a structural account: on current evidence, and with
the causal caveats of Section~\ref{sec:causes}, sycophancy behaves less like an isolated bug
than like a predictable consequence of optimizing for immediate human approval. RLHF introduces a systematic
preference bias that reward models amplify and policy optimization exploits, producing a family of
behaviors, outright agreement, selective omission, flattery, and capitulation under pressure, that
are statistically rational under the training objective and epistemically harmful in deployment.
Six integrative findings deserve emphasis.

First, sycophancy is not one behavior. Agreement and praise are causally separable in activation
space~\citep{vennemeyer2025notone}, and at least five subtypes are behaviorally
distinguishable~\citep{ye2026taxonomy}, so single-benchmark validation understates residual risk
(Section~\ref{subsec:appraisal}).

Second, it is domain-sensitive. Medical contexts produce the most acute
risks~\citep{fanous2025syceval,chen2025helpfulness}; tutors capitulate to authority and
face-saving pressure specifically~\citep{kasneci2026edu_safety}; psychiatric interaction is a harm
vector outside standard safety evaluation~\citep{auyeung2025psychogenic}; and financial advisory
sycophancy creates single-interlocutor echo chambers~\citep{li2026sycophancy_decisions}.
Certification must therefore be per-deployment, not per-model.

Third, the mitigation landscape is broader than the diagnosis but nowhere dominant. Activation
methods are precise and data-free but require white-box access and per-family mechanistic
knowledge~\citep{genadi2026linear,panickssery2023steering,hu2025monica}; data-level methods
generalize but need curated anti-sycophancy preference data~\citep{wei2023synthetic,
wang2024synthetic}; DPO sits between, contingent on curation quality. Every tier trades off
against helpfulness or capability somewhere (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigations}).

Fourth, sycophancy is a property of the human-AI system, not of the model alone. Its decision
effects depend on behavioral form~\citep{li2026sycophancy_decisions}, and brief interactions alter
offline behavior~\citep{cheng2025prosocial}, so model-side evaluation cannot bound system-level
harm.

Fifth, the evidentiary base warrants calibrated confidence. A substantial fraction of the corpus
is preprints without independent replication; many headline rates come from single evaluations of
proprietary, rapidly superseded model versions. The field needs preregistered benchmark studies, model-version reporting with checkpoint
identifiers, and cross-laboratory replication of the most-cited quantitative findings. We flag evidence status per source in the corpus coding (Appendix~\ref{app:openscience}).

Finally, mitigation faces a normative tension it cannot engineer away. A model that never agrees
is not helpful; one that always agrees is not trustworthy. Appropriate deference to expertise,
updating on new evidence, and rapport are legitimate behaviors that superficially resemble
sycophancy, and about one in ten user discussions values affirmation as emotional
support~\citep{noshin2026reddit}. Defining context-sensitive deference norms, training models to
enact them, and evaluating compliance at deployment are open problems requiring normative as well
as technical progress.
